# A project session's orb between its failures, the idle reaper and the
# person looking at why it failed. The child (the session's bough) starts
# the orb step by step and runs background jobs in it; serve's reaper
# stops an orb whose session has been quiet for BOUGH_ORB_IDLE; the
# thread page's "Why?" view names the failed phase, shows its log and
# offers its one fix (Rebuild image for a build, Edit resume.sh + Retry
# for setup, a link to the project's orb page for anything else).
# orb_lifecycle covers ownership, portals, Remove and the running
# snapshot; this spec leaves those out and adds jobs, the history clock
# and the failure-to-UI recovery loop.
#
# Where the code disagreed with the mapped model (and the code won):
#   - FailedAt has three answers, not five: build -> "build", resume.sh ->
#     "setup", sync, worktree and container all -> "start"
#     (internal/orb/state.go FailedAt). The child's start order is
#     prepare (sync + worktree) -> build -> container -> resume.sh.
#   - What a failure blocks depends on WHEN it happened, not on its
#     phase: a failure inside the first start (Prepare/Start, any step
#     before resume.sh) settles the row's handle with an error, and every
#     later command fails with that error until a new child starts
#     (plugins/orb/handle.go Command). A resume.sh failure settles the
#     handle well (Start returns nil), and so does a restart's failure
#     at "container" (ensureRunningLocked): the next command retries.
#     `opened` carries that.
#   - A job's output does not write history: only its start and its end
#     do (plugins/tools/jobs.go recordJob). A long quiet job therefore
#     does not keep its session from going idle.
#   - Every stop a job can die of here goes through MarkStopped first, and
#     StoppedSince also answers true when the container is simply not
#     running, so a job killed by ANY serve stop is recorded as "stopped
#     with the orb" and wakes nobody. The record is visible in the jobs
#     list; the agent is never told (KNOWN GAPS: ReaperNeverKillsJobs).
#   - Retry is a Stop orb (api.stopOrb) behind the failure view. Its
#     promise ("restarts and reruns resume.sh on the session's next
#     command") holds WITHOUT an owner as well: the next prompt makes
#     serve start a child, whose fresh start runs resume.sh
#     (RetryRerunsResume below). The suspected "no owner, nothing
#     restarts" did not survive.
#   - The prompt section for a build failure is failedPromptSection (the
#     start's error, whose "full log:" names build.log); promptSection's
#     "read resume.log" branch is reached only by a resume.sh failure,
#     because only that failure lets Start return nil. That suspected
#     bug did not survive either.
#   - A reaper stop that the runtime refuses restores the exact previous
#     state.json (orb.Restore) and changes nothing else: it is a
#     stutter, left out. BOUGH_ORB_IDLE=0 (reaper off) is a constant of
#     the serve, not a transition, and is left out too.
#
# Fields (role Orb):
#   file      state.json status: none | starting | running | failed | stopped
#   phase     state.json phase: prepare | build | container | resume ("" once ready)
#   ctr       the container: missing | stopped | running
#   owner     the session's child process lives
#   opened    the child's orb handle settled well (a later command can
#             restart the container); False while starting or after a
#             first-start failure
#   resuming  the child is inside resume.sh (it holds the orb's lock;
#             serve can still overwrite state.json under it)
#   idle      no history and no state.json write for BOUGH_ORB_IDLE
#   job       a background job runs in the container: none | running
#   jobend    how the last job ended, as the jobs list records it:
#             "" | exit (its own end, or "killed when bough quit" as the
#             child exits: recorded with its exit code) |
#             user | reaper (killed by that stop: "stopped with the orb",
#             no notice)
#   stopper   who wrote the last "stopped": "" (the child wrote since) |
#             user (Stop orb, Retry) | reaper | child (the session exited)
#   fail      where the child's last attempt really failed, "" once a new
#             attempt starts: the truth the page is checked against
#   blamed    the last resume.sh failure was a stop killing it, not the
#             script
#   script_bad  resume.sh exits non-zero; EditResumeSh fixes it
#   view      the thread's "Why?" view is toggled open (orbView "why");
#             it shows only while the row's status is failed
#   retry     a Retry was clicked and resume.sh has not rerun since

role Orb:
    action Init:
        self.file = "none"
        self.phase = ""
        self.ctr = "missing"
        self.owner = False
        self.opened = False
        self.resuming = False
        self.idle = False
        self.job = "none"
        self.jobend = ""
        self.stopper = ""
        self.fail = ""
        self.blamed = False
        self.script_bad = True
        self.view = False
        self.retry = False

    # --- the person and the session ---

    # A prompt to a session with no live child: serve starts one, and its
    # orb row runs a fresh start (Prepare writes starting(sync)).
    atomic action Prompt:
        require not self.owner
        self.owner = True
        self.opened = False
        self.file = "starting"
        self.phase = "prepare"
        self.child_wrote()

    # A turn writes history (a prompt to a live child, a command that ran
    # or failed with the start's error): the session is not idle.
    atomic action Activity:
        require self.owner and self.idle
        self.idle = False

    # The agent's next command after the container stopped behind the
    # child's back (Stop orb, Retry, the reaper) or after a restart
    # failed: ensureRunningLocked starts it again and reruns resume.sh.
    # A command while the container runs just runs (Activity): it does
    # NOT rerun a failed resume.sh, which is why Retry stops the orb.
    atomic action Command:
        require self.owner and self.opened and not in_flight(self) and self.ctr != "running"
        self.file = "starting"
        self.phase = "container"
        self.child_wrote()

    # tools.bash with a limit: a job in the container. Its start and its
    # end write history; its output does not.
    atomic action JobStarts:
        require self.owner and self.opened and not in_flight(self) and self.ctr == "running" and self.job == "none"
        self.job = "running"
        self.jobend = ""
        self.idle = False

    atomic action JobExits:
        require self.job == "running"
        self.job = "none"
        self.jobend = "exit"
        self.idle = False

    # The session's child exits (idle timeout, archive's clean path). An
    # open orb is stopped by the child itself (Orb.Stop writes stopped);
    # a start that failed leaves state.json as it was. Jobs die with it.
    atomic action OwnerExits:
        require self.owner and not in_flight(self)
        self.owner = False
        if self.job == "running":
            self.job = "none"
            self.jobend = "exit"
        if self.opened:
            self.opened = False
            self.file = "stopped"
            if self.ctr == "running":
                self.ctr = "stopped"
            self.stopper = "child"
            self.idle = False

    atomic action EditResumeSh:
        require self.script_bad
        self.script_bad = False

    # --- the child's start, step by step ---

    atomic fair action Prepared:
        require self.owner and self.file == "starting" and self.phase == "prepare"
        self.phase = "build"
        self.child_wrote()

    atomic fair action Built:
        require self.owner and self.file == "starting" and self.phase == "build"
        self.phase = "container"
        self.child_wrote()

    # A step before resume.sh fails: state.json failed at that phase.
    atomic action StartFails:
        require self.owner and self.file == "starting" and self.phase in ("prepare", "build", "container")
        self.file = "failed"
        self.fail = self.phase
        self.child_wrote()

    atomic fair action ContainerUp:
        require self.owner and self.file == "starting" and self.phase == "container"
        self.ctr = "running"
        self.phase = "resume"
        self.resuming = True
        self.retry = False
        self.child_wrote()

    # resume.sh passed: running, and the handle is open.
    atomic fair action ResumeOk:
        require self.resuming and not self.script_bad and self.ctr == "running"
        self.resuming = False
        self.opened = True
        self.file = "running"
        self.phase = ""
        self.child_wrote()

    # resume.sh exited non-zero, or was killed because the container
    # stopped under it: either way the child writes failed at resume.sh,
    # over whatever serve wrote meanwhile, and the handle is open.
    atomic fair action ResumeFails:
        require self.resuming and (self.script_bad or self.ctr != "running")
        self.blamed = self.ctr != "running"
        self.resuming = False
        self.opened = True
        self.file = "failed"
        self.phase = "resume"
        self.fail = "resume"
        self.child_wrote()

    # --- serve ---

    # Time passes with nothing written.
    atomic action GoIdle:
        require not self.idle and self.file in ("running", "failed")
        self.idle = True

    # The reaper: state.json running or failed and quiet for the limit,
    # whatever the jobs, the owner or the container (a missing one counts
    # as stopped).
    atomic fair action Reap:
        require self.idle and self.file in ("running", "failed")
        self.serve_stop("reaper")

    # Stop orb in the header, offered while the row is up. The confirm
    # it asks when jobs run is the page's, not a state.
    atomic action StopOrb:
        require orb_up(self)
        self.serve_stop("user")

    # --- the thread page's failure view ---

    atomic action OpenWhy:
        require status(self) == "failed" and not self.view
        self.view = True

    atomic action CloseWhy:
        require self.view
        self.view = False

    # Rebuild image (build failures only) rebuilds the project's image
    # for the NEXT start; this session's handle keeps its error.
    atomic action ClickRebuild:
        require shown(self) and failed_at(self) == "build"
        self.view = self.view

    # Retry (setup failures only): Stop orb behind the view.
    atomic action ClickRetry:
        require shown(self) and failed_at(self) == "setup"
        self.retry = True
        self.serve_stop("user")

    # Helpers are `atomic func` so a helper is never a yield point.
    # Every child write moves updatedAt: the idle clock restarts and the
    # last "stopped" is no longer serve's.
    atomic func child_wrote():
        self.idle = False
        self.stopper = ""
        if self.file == "starting":
            self.fail = ""
            self.blamed = False

    # Supervisor.stopOrb: MarkStopped (a write), stop the container, and
    # any job in it dies; its exit checks StoppedSince, which is true, so
    # it is recorded "stopped with the orb" and notifies nobody.
    atomic func serve_stop(who):
        if self.file != "none":
            self.file = "stopped"
        if self.ctr == "running":
            self.ctr = "stopped"
        if self.job == "running":
            self.job = "none"
            self.jobend = who
        self.stopper = who
        self.idle = False

# The child is mid-start: state.json starting, or inside resume.sh under
# a status serve overwrote.
def in_flight(o):
    return o.file == "starting" or o.resuming

# serve's orbState: running/starting need a live owner; failed and
# stopped are what the file says.
def status(o):
    if o.file in ("running", "starting") and not o.owner:
        return "stopped"
    return o.file

# orbUp on the page: the row says up (running trusts state.json; failed
# and starting ask the runtime, whose snapshot is taken as fresh here).
def orb_up(o):
    s = status(o)
    if s == "running":
        return True
    if o.file in ("running", "starting", "failed"):
        return o.ctr == "running"
    return False

# orb.FailedAt of state.json.
def failed_at(o):
    if o.file != "failed":
        return ""
    if o.phase == "build":
        return "build"
    if o.phase == "resume":
        return "setup"
    return "start"

# orb.LogFor: build.log for a build failure, else resume.log.
def log_for(o):
    if failed_at(o) == "build":
        return "build.log"
    return "resume.log"

# OrbFailureBody's buttons for a phase.
def fix_for(o):
    p = failed_at(o)
    if p == "build":
        return "rebuild"
    if p == "setup":
        return "retry"
    return "link"

# The failure view is on screen.
def shown(o):
    return o.view and status(o) == "failed"

# What a real failure at a step needs: its FailedAt word, the log that
# explains it and its fix.
def truth_phase(step):
    if step == "build":
        return "build"
    if step == "resume":
        return "setup"
    return "start"

def truth_log(step):
    if step == "build":
        return "build.log"
    return "resume.log"

def truth_fix(step):
    if step == "build":
        return "rebuild"
    if step == "resume":
        return "retry"
    return "link"

action Init:
    orb = Orb()

# The view names the phase the child really failed in, shows the log that
# explains it (build.log for a build) and offers only that phase's fix.
always assertion ViewMatchesFailure:
    return not shown(orb) or (orb.fail != ""
        and failed_at(orb) == truth_phase(orb.fail)
        and log_for(orb) == truth_log(orb.fail)
        and fix_for(orb) == truth_fix(orb.fail))

# Retry's promise: after it, the orb never shows running again without
# resume.sh having run again — whether the next command reaches the
# child that failed or a new one serve starts for the next prompt.
always assertion RetryRerunsResume:
    return not (orb.retry and orb.file == "running")

# Only a stop hides a failure: nothing the child or the page does turns a
# failed orb into anything but failed until a new attempt starts. (Which
# stop is allowed to is the KNOWN GAP ReapKeepsFailure.)
always assertion OnlyAStopHidesAFailure:
    return orb.fail == "" or orb.file == "failed" or orb.stopper != ""

# No job outlives the container it ran in: every stop ends the jobs in
# it and records how (the jobs list's "stopped with the orb"), so a
# reaped job is never simply gone. (That nobody is told is the KNOWN GAP
# ReaperNeverKillsJobs.)
always assertion NoJobOutlivesItsContainer:
    return orb.job == "none" or orb.ctr == "running"

# A child's start never hangs: every step ends in running or failed.
always eventually assertion StartsSettle:
    return not in_flight(orb)

# Every idle running or failed orb is eventually stopped.
always eventually assertion IdleOrbsReaped:
    return not (orb.idle and orb.file in ("running", "failed"))

# KNOWN GAPS (not asserted; each was added to a copy of this spec and
# fizz failed it on the trace shown).
#
# ReaperNeverKillsJobs: the reaper has no guard for running jobs (the
# page's Stop orb asks first; reapIdleOrbs does not look), a job's output
# does not count as activity, and the job's end is recorded "stopped with
# the orb" with no notice, so the agent never learns a four-hour build or
# a dev server died:
#   always assertion ReaperNeverKillsJobs:
#       return orb.jobend != "reaper"
# fizz: Prompt, Prepared, Built, ContainerUp, ResumeFails, JobStarts,
# GoIdle, Reap (a job in a setup-failed orb; a running one is the same).
#
# ReapKeepsFailure: reaping a failed orb runs MarkStopped: status
# "stopped", FailedAt "", LogFor falls back to resume.log and the failure
# view disappears. After a first-start failure (sync, worktree, build or
# container) the session is then shown "stopped" although its next
# command fails with the old error and nothing restarts it:
#   always assertion ReapKeepsFailure:
#       return orb.fail == "" or orb.file == "failed" or orb.stopper in ("user", "child")
# fizz: Prompt, StartFails (at prepare), GoIdle, Reap.
#
# StopNeverBlamedOnResume: Stop orb is offered while resume.sh runs (the
# container is up), and the child then records the killed script as a
# resume.sh failure over serve's "stopped": the page says "Setup failed"
# and offers Retry for a stop the person made:
#   always assertion StopNeverBlamedOnResume:
#       return not (orb.file == "failed" and orb.phase == "resume" and orb.blamed)
# fizz: Prompt, Prepared, Built, ContainerUp, StopOrb, ResumeFails.
#
# Not modelled, seen in the code: for a "start" failure (sync, worktree,
# container) the view's "Full resume.log" is resume.log, which that
# failure never wrote (it is empty or a previous start's); and the
# "Stopped. The orb restarts…" note after Retry unmounts with the view as
# soon as the list poll reads the stopped status.
